{% block summary %}
<p>The OSLO Transport Nodes application profile shows how to use terms from the corresponding <a href="https://data.vlaanderen.be/ns/mobiliteit/vervoersknooppunten">vocabulary</a> to exchange data on Transport Nodes for passenger transport.</p>

<p>Because of the diverse offer and the transfer options, transport hubs where passengers can transfer play an important role in achieving basic accessibility.</p>

<p>The model consists of two parts. The first part provides a general definition of a Transport Hub and describes the elements that make it up with specific attention to passenger transport. The Passenger uses Transport Nodes for his Journey or Route and this is described in part two.</p>

<p>Below we focus on part one, for more information about part two we refer to the <a href="https://data.vlaanderen.be/doc/applicatieprofiel/mobiliteit-trips-en-aanbod">application profile OSLO Mobility: Trips and Offer</a>.</p>

<p>Transport hubs consist of 1) Infrastructure elements, 2) Transport networks, 3) Mobility services and 4) Additional services.</p>

<p>The Transport Hub class will be further specialised into a Hoppin Point, which is the Flemish version of a Transport Hub.</p>

<p>Infrastructure elements represent the physical aspect of a Transport Node, it concerns objects such as Information Columns (such as the Hoppin Column), Stops, Car Parks, Bicycle Sheds, Chargers, etc. that are necessary for the existing Transport Networks and the Mobility or Additional Services offered at the Transport Node. The Infrastructure Elements and their specializations come from the vocabalaria <a href="https://data.vlaanderen.be/doc/vocabularium/openbaardomein/infrastructuur">OSLO Public Domain - Infrastructure Elements</a>, <a href="https://data.vlaanderen.be/doc/applicatieprofiel/mobiliteit/dienstregeling-en-planning/stopplaatsen/kandidaatstandaard/2021-11-18">OSLO Mobility - Timetable and Planning - Stops</a> and from the <a href="https://www.mobivoc.org/">Open Mobility Vocabulary</a>.</p>

<p>The Transport networks present on a Transport Hub are accessible via Transport Objects such as Transport nodes (stops, stations, etc.) and Transport connections between those nodes. Virtual Nodes (points located on a connection between two Transport Nodes, e.g. the position of an abandoned shared scooter or the address of a Traveller) are also possible. The Transport object class and its subclasses are reused from the <a href="https://data.vlaanderen.be/ns/transportnetwerk">OSLO Transport Network</a> vocabulary.</p>

<p>Mobility services are primarily available via the existing Infrastructure Elements and Transport Objects. These can be scheduled services or shared scooters, but also parking spaces or bicycle pumps. A Mobility Service may include other Mobility Services, e.g. in the case of the Hoppin Central which integrates several services into one service, but also in the case of a Provider which describes its services at different levels of granularity (e.g. at a high level, the "shared mobility in Flanders" service and at a lower level, the "shared cars in Antwerp" and "shared bicycles in Ghent" services). The class Mobility Service and dependent classes come from the vocabulary <a href="https://data.vlaanderen.be/ns/mobiliteit/trips-en-aanbod">OSLO-Mobility: Trips and Offer</a>.</p>

<p>At a Transport Hub we typically also find all kinds of Additional Services, including services that save the Traveller additional travel such as post boxes, bakeries, newsagents, etc.</p>

<p>Enumerations are in principle described outside the application profile, so the enumeration values shown in the diagram are illustrative.</p>

